schema_id: atlas.rapids-corpus-ref
schema_version: 1
schema_kind: gpu_corpus_reference
status: ACTIVE

description: >
  Revisioned reference to a semantic_768 corpus snapshot for cuVS/CAGRA. This is
  the preferred large-corpus transport shape; inline JSON corpus vectors remain a
  tiny-fixture compatibility path only.

fields:
  schema: { type: literal, value: atlas.rapids-corpus-ref.v1 }
  corpusRefId: { type: string, required: true }
  representationId: { type: literal, value: semantic_768 }
  representationRevision: { type: string, required: true }
  workspaceRevision: { type: string, required: true }
  featureRevision: { type: string, required: true }
  rowCount: { type: integer, minimum: 1 }
  dimension: { type: literal, value: 768 }
  dtype:
    type: enum
    values: [float32, float16]
  metric: { type: literal, value: cosine }
  dataRefId: { type: string, required: true }
  identityRefId: { type: string, required: true }
  ordinalRefId: { type: string, required: true }
  checksum: { type: string, required: true }
  createdAt: { type: datetime, required: true }
  producerRevision: { type: string, required: true }

invariants:
  - row ordinal must round-trip to packetKey/sourceRevision identity without relying on vector-database point IDs
  - metric is cosine because this snapshot belongs to canonical semantic_768
  - cuVS brute-force is exact oracle; CAGRA consumes the same corpus identity but remains ANN
  - large corpus execution should pass dataRefId/identityRefId rather than inline JSON float arrays
  - Arrow IPC and mmap files may carry bytes, but serialized bytes do not become canonical identity
